   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to direct the department of education to study the frequency of\nresidents who are being assessed library taxes for more than one library\ndistrict and to make recommendations to prevent the double taxation of\nresidents; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration\nthereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S7857",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7857-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7857\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to direct the department of education to study the frequency of\nresidents who are being assessed library taxes for more than one library\ndistrict and to make recommendations to prevent the double taxation of\nresidents; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon expira-\ntion thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nto study the problem of double library taxation statewide and develop\nrecommendations to address it.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Directs the state education department to conduct a study of\nthe frequency of residents who are being assessed library taxes for more\nthan one library district. The study will include:\n\n-The estimated number of residents paying for more than one district and\nhow much they are paying;\n\n-The economic impact on library districts that would result if residents\nwere prohibited from being assessed for more than one district; and\n\n-Ways the economic impact upon library districts could be minimized.\n\nSection 2: Directs that the commissioner of education shall submit a\nreport to the governor, the temporary president of the senate, and\nspeaker of the assembly on or before December 31, 2023.\n\nSection 3: Sets effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThere are three types of library districts: those based upon municipal\nboundaries; those based on school district boundaries; and those with\nspecial/legislative boundaries. Many residents throughout the state pay\ntwice for the same library service by living in areas where a munici-\npality's library district overlaps with a school district's boundary.\nThere have been numerous legislative attempts to solve this problem but\nnone have been enacted. By analyzing the scope of the problem and its\npotential economic impacts, this bill would pave the way for a statewide\nsolution to the problem.\n\nDouble library taxation is an inherently unfair situation for those\nresidents who, by no fault of their own, live in overlapping library\ndistricts.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2018: A11015\n\n2019: S5522A/A7441A-vetoed by Governor (VM-166)\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nMinimal cost to state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThe act shall take effect immediately and shall expire and be deemed\nrepealed January 31, 2024.\n",
