    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7857A\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act in relation to requiring the commissioner of taxation and finance\nto study the frequency of residents who are being assessed library taxes\nfor more than one library and to make recommendations to prevent the\ndouble taxation of residents; and providing for the repeal of such\nprovisions upon expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo study the problem of double library taxation statewide and develop\nrecommendations to address it.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Requires the Commissioner of Taxation and Finance to conduct\na study of the frequency of residents who are being assessed library\ntaxes for more than one public library or association library. The study\nmust include:\n\n-The estimated number of residents paying taxes for more than one public\nlibrary or association library and the amount they are paying in library\ntaxes;\n\n-The estimated economic impact on public libraries and association\nlibraries that would result if residents were prohibited from being\nassessed for more than one public library or association library; and\n\n-Ways to minimize the economic impact upon public library or association\nlibraries resulting from the prohibition\n\nSection 2: Requires the commissioner of taxation and finance to submit a\nreport to the governor, the temporary president of the senate, and\nspeaker of the assembly one year after the effective date of this act.\n\nSection 3: Sets effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThere are three types of library districts: those based upon municipal\nboundaries; those based on school district boundaries; and those with\nspecial/legislative boundaries. Many residents throughout the state pay\ntwice for the same library service by living in areas where a munici-\npality's library district overlaps with a school district's boundary.\nThere have been numerous legislative attempts to solve this problem, but\nnone have been enacted. By analyzing the scope of the problem and its\npotential economic impacts, this bill would pave the way for a statewide\nsolution to the problem.\n\nDouble library taxation is an inherently unfair situation for those\nresidents who, by no fault of their own, live in overlapping library\ndistricts.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nMinimal cost to state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThe act shall take effect immediately and shall expire and be deemed\nrepealed January 31, 2024.\n",
